<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Wild bytes ~ the frame decides at the edge, the reader tolerates outside it

''Two seats meet bytes the house never framed, and they hold different offices.''

* ''At the disk→records edge'', the ingest gate reads the FRAME, never the author: `bare` carries whole;
  `torn` refuses and leaves the file where it stands; `stale` warns and still decides.
* ''Outside every bag, wiki, doc and carrier'', a READER over text the house never authored (a month of
  transcripts) tolerates every grammar it meets and names that tolerance at its seat.

The house CARRIES wild bytes FLAGGED — a bare carrier lands whole as readable text, its demotion named on
the receipt — and never repair-tolerates state it can re-found.

> ''At the edge the frame decides; outside the edge the reader tolerates; nowhere does a gate guess who wrote the bytes.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-line>>

!! The line ~ by FRAME at the edge, by SEAT outside it

''At the edge, the frame verdict decides'' (`packages/lararium-memetic-frame/src/verdict.ts`, the
`FrameVerdict` union; policy composed in `packages/lararium-tw5/src/ingest-gate.ts`):

|! verdict |! what the gate does |
| `bare` | carried whole as ONE `text/plain` record, never parsed, never refused; WARNING on the receipt (OWED: a mark on the record) |
| `torn` | REFUSED; the bytes stay on disk; nothing rewrites them |
| `stale` | WARNING; the edit still gets a real decision — noop, ingest, or conflict |
| `match` · `absent` | decided — the parse runs and the gate rules noop, ingest, or conflict |

''Across the layers, the law holds one seat each:''

|! layer |! what the wild-bytes law asks of it |
| meme on disk | the gate reads the frame; re-make means RENDER from records |
| CRDT doc | the record seat; holds the bare record; the mark stays OWED; the doc paves only by the founding rite, and that pave retires with the alpha |
| bag | the law's home as admission policy per grade — canon bags admit framed carriers alone; a capture bag may admit bare, flagged; unbuilt, OWED |
| wiki | the law does not apply: a recipe holds no bytes; the cascade routes and never decides whether to write |
| reader over outside text | tolerates, scoped, outside the four layers |

A gate MUST NOT read provenance it cannot observe; it reads the frame.
A reader of outside text MAY tolerate a retired shape, scoped to that reader, its docblock naming the wild
source.
A store layer MUST NOT carry a reading alias for a house-authored anchor.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/parse-dont-validate>>

!! Parse, don't validate ~ the edge types the bytes once

''A reader turns wild bytes into a typed reading at the edge, exactly once'' (Alexis King, //Parse, don't
validate//). Everything past the edge holds the typed reading and never re-inspects the raw bytes; a
validator that answers yes-or-no and hands the raw bytes onward leaves every later reader to re-decide, and
each one decides a little differently.

A reading the edge cannot type takes no default shape. Three floors hold it at three grains, each a
demotion the operator sees and never a drop:

* ''FRAME floor'' — a bare carrier holds as one record.
* ''BODY floor'' — a malformed span wraps as water
  (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing#/recovery-policy">>).
* ''AHU floor'' — un-decomposable content fences
  (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor">>); RULED, UNBUILT.

A reader names which floor it stands on. Water never lies about structure.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-alpha-line>>

!! With the alpha line ~ one law seen from two sides

''The alpha line rules no back-compat and no shims'' for anything the house authored: a ruling that obsoletes
a shape deletes the old reading whole. Its standing text lives in the ''Two LAWS above the code'' line
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/handoff#/shift-identity-watch">>) and the standing law's
''Early alpha'' entry (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff#/standing-law">>); the alpha line
awaits its own carrier.

A pre-ruling shape meets its layer's own act, never a second reading:

* a disk carrier re-renders from records;
* a doc paves only by the founding rite, and that pave retires with the alpha;
* a bag re-mints a new doc under a new pointer;
* a wiki re-mints its record and strands its fingerprint-keyed mounts.

''Wild bytes mark the one place that line does not reach'', and the reason stays structural rather than
lenient: no rite re-makes a transcript somebody already spoke, so no layer act applies. The two laws
partition the corpus and never overlap — ''authored → the layer's act; wild → carry and flag''. A
tolerance found anywhere else reads as a shim wearing this law's name.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! Prior art ~ the robustness principle, measured

* ''RFC 9413, Maintaining Robust Protocols'' (Thomson & Schinazi) — the IETF's own reckoning with
  Postel's //be liberal in what you accept//. Tolerance of malformed input does not stay tolerance: peers
  come to depend on it, the protocol's real grammar drifts away from its written one, and the cure names
  ACTIVE maintenance — specify the error handling, and keep the tolerated set from growing.
* ''Hyrum's law'' — with enough consumers, every observable behaviour of an interface gets depended on.
  A tolerance a reader extends to re-foundable state becomes, in time, a contract nobody wrote. Scoping
  tolerance to bytes the house cannot re-make bounds what Hyrum's law can capture.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/standing-instance>>

!! The standing instance ~ a reader, outside the four layers

The harvester (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/turn-harvest.ts`) reads `.jsonl` transcripts and lands
drawers in mempalace; no bag, wiki, doc or carrier holds those bytes, which keeps this tolerance from ever
reaching a gate. It reads a month of real turns written under whatever the frame said that week, and keeps
its retired-head tolerance — a retired sigil head stays readable there — under this law: no rite re-makes
those transcripts, and refusing a retired head would read a turn that fired its instrument as a turn that
fired nothing. Its own docblock states the same cut: //a corpus states one grammar; a reader tolerates
every grammar it will meet//. Canon bags admit framed carriers alone; the pre-commit frame check holds that
line, and no bag-grade policy yet enforces it in the sink.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/owed>>

!! Owed

* ''A durable mark on a bare record'' — a field or meta the record carries, so the demotion survives sync
  and projection rather than living on one receipt.
* ''Admission policy by bag grade, at the sink'' — canon bags admit framed carriers alone; a capture bag
  may admit bare, flagged.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

!! Edges

